Let $f(x)=-\frac{(x-3)^2}{\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{-x^{2}+6x-9}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=-x^{\frac{3}{2}}+6x^{\frac{1}{2}}-9x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=-\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}+3x^{-\frac{1}{2}}+\frac{9}{2}x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(4,-\frac{1}{2}\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(4)&=-\frac{3}{2}\left(4\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}+3\left(4\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}+\frac{9}{2}\left(4\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=-3+\frac{3}{2}+\frac{9}{16}\\
&=-\frac{15}{16}
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(-\frac{15}{16}\right)}\\
&=\frac{16}{15}
\end{aligned}
$$

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 2 x^{\frac{1}{4}} - 3, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{1}{2 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}=- \frac{1}{2 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(16)&=- \frac{1}{2 (16)^{\frac{3}{4}}}=- \frac{1}{16}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{1}{16}\right)}=16.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(16,-7)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-7)&=16(x-(16)), \\y&=16 x - 263.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=16x+(-263), \\x&=\frac{263}{16}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{263}{16},0\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 3 x^{\frac{1}{2}} - 2, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{3}{2 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}=- \frac{3}{2 \sqrt{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(1)&=- \frac{3}{2 \sqrt{(1)}}=- \frac{3}{2}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{3}{2}\right)}=\frac{2}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(1,-5)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-5)&=\frac{2}{3}(x-(1)), \\y&=\frac{2 x}{3} - \frac{17}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=\frac{2}{3}\cdot 0+\left(- \frac{17}{3}\right), \\y&=- \frac{17}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(0,- \frac{17}{3}\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=x^{\frac{3}{2}} - 1, \\f'(x)&=\frac{3 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}{2}=\frac{3 \sqrt{x}}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=\frac{3 \sqrt{(4)}}{2}=3, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(3)}=- \frac{1}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,7)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(7)&=- \frac{1}{3}(x-(4)), \\y&=- \frac{x}{3} + \frac{25}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=- \frac{1}{3}x+\left(\frac{25}{3}\right), \\x&=25.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(25,0)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=3 x^{\frac{2}{3}} - 3, \\f'(x)&=\frac{2}{x^{\frac{1}{3}}}=\frac{2}{\sqrt[3]{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(8)&=\frac{2}{\sqrt[3]{(8)}}=1, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(1)}=-1.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(8,9)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(9)&=-1(x-(8)), \\y&=- x + 17.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=-1\cdot 0+(17), \\y&=17.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(0,17)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=2 + \frac{1}{x}, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{1}{x^{2}}=- \frac{1}{x^{2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(2)&=- \frac{1}{(2)^{2}}=- \frac{1}{4}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{1}{4}\right)}=4.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $\left(2,\frac{5}{2}\right)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(\frac{5}{2}\right)&=4(x-(2)), \\y&=4 x - \frac{11}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=4x+\left(- \frac{11}{2}\right), \\x&=\frac{11}{8}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{11}{8},0\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=x^{\frac{1}{4}} + 2, \\f'(x)&=\frac{1}{4 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}=\frac{1}{4 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(16)&=\frac{1}{4 (16)^{\frac{3}{4}}}=\frac{1}{32}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{1}{32}\right)}=-32.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(16,4)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(4)&=-32(x-(16)), \\y&=- 32 x + 516.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=-32\cdot 0+(516), \\y&=516.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(0,516)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=3 x^{\frac{1}{2}} + 2, \\f'(x)&=\frac{3}{2 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}=\frac{3}{2 \sqrt{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=\frac{3}{2 \sqrt{(4)}}=\frac{3}{4}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)}=- \frac{4}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,8)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(8)&=- \frac{4}{3}(x-(4)), \\y&=- \frac{4 x}{3} + \frac{40}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=- \frac{4}{3}x+\left(\frac{40}{3}\right), \\x&=10.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(10,0)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 2 x^{\frac{3}{2}} + 2, \\f'(x)&=- 3 x^{\frac{1}{2}}=- 3 \sqrt{x}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=- 3 \sqrt{(4)}=-6, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(-6)}=\frac{1}{6}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,-14)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-14)&=\frac{1}{6}(x-(4)), \\y&=\frac{x}{6} - \frac{44}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=\frac{1}{6}\cdot 0+\left(- \frac{44}{3}\right), \\y&=- \frac{44}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(0,- \frac{44}{3}\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 3 x^{\frac{2}{3}} - 2, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{2}{x^{\frac{1}{3}}}=- \frac{2}{\sqrt[3]{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(1)&=- \frac{2}{\sqrt[3]{(1)}}=-2, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(-2)}=\frac{1}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(1,-5)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-5)&=\frac{1}{2}(x-(1)), \\y&=\frac{x}{2} - \frac{11}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=\frac{1}{2}x+\left(- \frac{11}{2}\right), \\x&=11.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(11,0)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=-2 + \frac{1}{x}, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{1}{x^{2}}=- \frac{1}{x^{2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(1)&=- \frac{1}{(1)^{2}}=-1, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(-1)}=1.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(1,-1)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-1)&=1(x-(1)), \\y&=x - 2.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=1\cdot 0+(-2), \\y&=-2.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(0,-2)$.

